{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And every meal-offering, etc.</b> The verse says the Memorial of five meal-offerings, to exclude five things: (1) that the sons of Aharon should not divide [them] by ancestral house — animal offerings corresponding to meal-offerings — but rather each one takes his portion in the meal-offering. (2) That they should not divide bird-offerings corresponding to meal-offerings, even though they are equivalent in one respect, for we find that a bird offering comes in poverty and the meal-offering stands in their stead at the extreme of poverty. (3) That they should not divide bird-offerings corresponding to animal offerings, even though both are kinds of blood. (4) That they should not divide meal-offerings corresponding to meal-offerings, even though they are kinds of flour. (5) That they should not divide even a machavat against a machavat, and a marcheshet against a marcheshet, even though their manner of preparation is the same." "And although the exclusions are meal-offerings, they interpreted them by the principle of 'if it is not needed for its own topic.' But I saw the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aseh HaKorbanot, chapter 10, halakhah 15) who wrote in a different manner, for from the fact that the verse distinguished between the baked meal-offerings and the meal-offering of fine flour — for regarding baked [offerings] it says 'כָּל הַמִּנְחָה לַכֹּהֵן הַמַּקְרִיב' etc., and regarding the fine-flour meal-offering it says 'וְכָל מִנְחָה לְכָל בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן' etc. — this tells you that even the fine-flour meal-offering, which one might have thought they would divide meal-offering against meal-offering, given that if they divided it among them this one would receive a handful's worth and so forth, which is unfit neither to knead nor to bake — even so, to all the sons of Aharon they divide it on its own. From here the Sages said: one does not divide a meal-offering against a meal-offering, end quote. You thus find according to his words that the derivation is from the verse's statement 'לְכָל בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן' in the fine-flour meal-offering; from this we learn for all the meal-offerings, and from the baraita this is not heard." "<b>Further,</b> the Rambam was precise in his language that the derivation is from the fact that the verse distinguished them, implying that if it had not distinguished them we would not have heard this — unlike the baraita. And perhaps the Rambam holds that from the fact that the verse distinguished between the baked meal-offerings etc., in this the verse revealed to us its intent, which is to say that they should not divide among them one against the other, even in the fine-flour meal-offering, and from this they derived [the rule] for all meal-offerings. For regarding the marcheshet and the machavat, no distinction was mentioned regarding 'all,' but only that they should not take [it] other than the ancestral house of that day; and from where do you say that they should not take a machavat against a marcheshet, if no order of its distribution was mentioned in it — whether to all of them together or to one of them? And what they derived for all five particulars from the five exclusions is an asmakhta, and in any case the primary derivation is from the verse's having distinguished." "And now one needs to know why the verse specified all the meal-offerings, since our derivation is from what it distinguished — should it not have said only 'כָּל מִנְחָה לַכֹּהֵן הַמַּקְרִיב' etc. and 'כָּל מִנְחָה חֲרֵבָה לְכָל בְּנֵי' etc.? And perhaps it is that after the verse descended to specify ma'afeh and choreivah, it was concerned lest a person err to distinguish between them and the other meal-offerings. We also find that they of blessed memory derived (Torat Kohanim; Menachot 63a) 'וְכָל נַעֲשָׂה בַמַּרְחֶשֶׁת וְעַל מַחֲבַת' — that they are called by the names of their vessels, and the practical consequence is: one who says 'הֲרֵי עָלַי מַרְחֶשֶׁת' — has vowed [to bring] a service vessel. And this is a matter disputed (Menachot 63a) between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel. Therefore the verse said <b>וְכָל נַעֲשָׂה בַמַּרְחֶשֶׁת וְעַל מַחֲבַת</b> to reveal to you that they are called by the names of their vessels. And the verse's saying <b>אֲשֶׁר תֵּאָפֶה בַתַּנּוּר</b> — Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehudah also derived that it comes to teach about itself from the juxtaposition of 'כָּל נַעֲשָׂה בַמַּרְחֶשֶׁת וְעַל מַחֲבַת': just as the marcheshet and the machavat are two, so too a ma'afeh tavachur is two, and one may not bring half as challot and half as rekikin. And also to teach about the marcheshet and the machavat, it comes to say: just as the oven is a vessel, so too the machavat and the marcheshet are vessels, as stated there in Torat Kohanim. And there remains the statement 'כָּל מִנְחָה בְּלוּלָה בַשֶּׁמֶן וַחֲרֵבָה' — why was it necessary to state two?" "<b>And it would appear</b> that the intent of Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehudah, who derived that every meal-offering of ma'afeh constitutes two kinds of meal-offerings, learned this only from the fact that the verse stated twice 'כָּל' afterward — and they are two and two. And therefore he is precise in his language: the derivation is to say 'וְכָל מִנְחָה' 'וְכָל נַעֲשָׂה' etc. 'וְכָל מִנְחָה בְּלוּלָה' etc. — just as the 'כָּל' and 'כָּל' stated below are two kinds etc. And therefore, if the verse had said 'וְכָל מִנְחָה בְּלוּלָה' alone or 'כָּל מִנְחָה חֲרֵבָה' alone, there would be no room to derive [anything], and the disputant would have grounds to object and say: 'כָּל מִנְחָה חֲרֵבָה' proves [it], for it says 'כָּל' yet there is only one kind — so too a ma'afeh tavachur is one kind. For this reason it was necessary to state the 'כָּל' stated in the verse."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:05.801183846Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}